      "title": "Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease — Overview",
      "detailsHtml": "<p><strong>What it is and why it matters:</strong> Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is a chronic disorder in which stomach contents repeatedly flow into the esophagus, producing symptoms and/or complications. Untreated chronic reflux can cause esophagitis, peptic strictures, Barrett's esophagus and increase the risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Who is commonly affected & key clinical idea:</strong> GERD is common in Western populations (about 10–20% overall; 18–28% reported in North America) and is associated with obesity, pregnancy, smoking, hiatal hernia, and certain medications. The central pathophysiology is failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to prevent reflux; management focuses on symptom control, preventing complications, and identifying alarm features that require investigation.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Typical symptoms:</strong> heartburn, regurgitation, acidic taste, dysphagia; extraesophageal symptoms include chronic cough, hoarseness, and laryngitis.</li>\n  <li><strong>Initial management:</strong> lifestyle changes (weight loss, avoid lying down after meals, elevate head of bed, avoid triggers), antacids/H2 blockers or proton‑pump inhibitors for symptomatic control.</li>\n  <li><strong>When to investigate:</strong> poor response to therapy or alarm features — consider endoscopy, pH monitoring, or manometry as appropriate.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Red flag</span><p>Dysphagia, unintentional weight loss, anaemia, gastrointestinal bleeding, or persistent vomiting require urgent evaluation (endoscopy and further workup).</p></div>\n",
