      "title": "Causes & Risk Factors",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>GERD results from recurrent reflux of stomach contents into the esophagus, most commonly due to impaired closure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). Mechanical factors that increase intra‑abdominal pressure or disrupt the gastroesophageal junction also promote reflux.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Modifiable risks</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Obesity — higher BMI increases esophageal acid exposure and symptom severity.</li>\n      <li>Smoking and alcohol use.</li>\n      <li>Dietary triggers that relax the LES or delay gastric emptying: coffee, chocolate, fatty foods, acidic or spicy foods.</li>\n      <li>Certain medications that reduce LES tone or worsen reflux: benzodiazepines, calcium‑channel blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, some asthma drugs, and NSAIDs.</li>\n      <li>Pregnancy — transient hormonal and mechanical effects (weight and uterine pressure).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Non‑modifiable / structural risks</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Hiatal hernia — disrupts the gastroesophageal junction and promotes reflux.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Associated or less certain factors</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Obstructive sleep apnea, gallstones, and links with H. pylori have been reported but are not conclusive.</li>\n      <li>Mast cell activation has been described in some cases.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>In practice, weight reduction, stopping smoking, avoiding LES‑relaxing drugs when possible, and addressing a hiatal hernia (if symptomatic or refractory) are the highest‑yield interventions to reduce reflux.</p></div>",
      "visualPrompt": "Diagram showing LES incompetence and hiatal hernia with arrows for reflux; labels for obesity, smoking, and LES‑relaxing medications as common drivers.",
      "aiEdited": true
