      "title": "Overview",
      "detailsHtml": "<p><strong>What</strong>: Malaria is an acute, sometimes life‑threatening, mosquito‑borne infection caused by Plasmodium parasites. <strong>Why it matters</strong>: it causes substantial global morbidity and mortality, particularly in young children in sub‑Saharan Africa, and delays in diagnosis or species‑appropriate treatment increase risk of severe complications. <strong>Who</strong>: people living in or returning from endemic tropical/subtropical areas and nonimmune travelers. <strong>Key clinical idea</strong>: fever in someone with recent travel to an endemic area should prompt urgent evaluation for malaria.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Transmission</strong>: spread by bites of infected female Anopheles mosquitoes; parasites mature in liver then infect red blood cells.</li>\n  <li><strong>Typical timeline & symptoms</strong>: symptoms commonly begin ~10–15 days after exposure (can be later); typical: fever, chills, headache, malaise, nausea. Relapses can occur with P. vivax and P. ovale due to dormant liver hypnozoites.</li>\n  <li><strong>Species & severity</strong>: P. falciparum causes the majority of deaths; P. vivax common outside Africa; P. knowlesi is a zoonotic cause; P. malariae and P. ovale often milder but clinically important.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diagnosis</strong>: confirm with blood microscopy (thick/thin films) or antigen rapid diagnostic tests; PCR used in specialized settings.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention & treatment</strong>: bite prevention (nets, repellents, vector control), chemoprophylaxis for travelers, and species/geography‑guided antimalarial therapy (resistance patterns affect drug choice). WHO‑endorsed vaccines now complement prevention efforts.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Red flag</span><p>Signs of severe malaria (medical emergency): impaired consciousness or seizures, severe anemia, respiratory distress, jaundice/renal failure, blackwater urine, or shock. Rapid referral to hospital is essential.</p></div>",
