      "title": "Type 2 Diabetes — Overview",
      "detailsHtml": "<p><strong>What it is and why it matters</strong>: Type 2 diabetes is a chronic metabolic disorder defined by hyperglycemia from insulin resistance plus a relative deficiency of insulin. It is the most common form of diabetes and a leading cause of cardiovascular disease, kidney failure, blindness, peripheral vascular disease and lower‑limb amputation. Prevalence has risen sharply with obesity and sedentary lifestyles and the condition shortens life expectancy by about a decade in many populations. Key clinical idea: recognise risk early (often asymptomatic), confirm with blood testing, prioritise lifestyle interventions, and escalate pharmacotherapy (metformin → additional agents → insulin) while actively screening for and managing complications.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Typical presentation and diagnosis</strong>: Symptoms often develop slowly and many people are asymptomatic. Common symptoms are polyuria, polydipsia, fatigue and unexplained weight change; neuropathic symptoms and poor wound healing are frequent. Diagnostic thresholds: fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L), 2‑hour OGTT ≥200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L), random glucose ≥200 mg/dL with symptoms, or HbA1c ≥6.5% (48 mmol/mol).</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major risk factors</strong>: overweight/obesity, physical inactivity, age, family history/genetic predisposition; some ethnic groups at higher risk; prior gestational diabetes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Management priorities</strong>: intensive lifestyle change (weight loss, regular physical activity, reduce sugar‑sweetened drinks), metformin as first‑line pharmacotherapy for most, add agents guided by comorbidities and glucose targets; many patients ultimately require insulin.</li>\n  <li><strong>Monitoring</strong>: routine HbA1c and complication screening (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, cardiovascular risk); glucose self‑monitoring emphasized for patients on insulin or with hypoglycemia risk.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention & screening</strong>: lifestyle programs prevent or delay progression in prediabetes. Screening recommendations in the source: USPSTF (screen adults 35–70 years who are overweight/obese; lower BMI threshold for Asian adults); ADA guidance (broader screening starting at age 35 and for adults with BMI ≥25 or ≥23 in Asian Americans with additional risk factors).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Red flag</span><p>Very high blood glucose with decreasing consciousness (hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state) or signs of sepsis/poor perfusion in a person with diabetes require urgent hospital assessment. Slow‑healing foot ulcers and progressive ischemia demand prompt vascular and podiatric evaluation to reduce amputation risk.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient education</span><p>Modest weight loss and regular physical activity substantially lower risk and improve control. Reduce sugary drinks, follow a balanced diet, and attend regular checks (blood glucose/HbA1c, eyes, kidneys, feet).</p></div>",
