      "title": "Causes & Risk Factors",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>Type 2 diabetes arises from insulin resistance plus progressive beta‑cell dysfunction. The dominant, modifiable drivers are excess adiposity and physical inactivity; obesity (commonly defined as BMI &gt;25) is the single most important preventable risk.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Modifiable risks</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Overweight/obesity and central adiposity (high waist–hip ratio)</li>\n      <li>Physical inactivity and sedentary behaviour</li>\n      <li>Unhealthy diet — excess sugar‑sweetened drinks, high saturated/trans fats, very high white‑rice intake</li>\n      <li>Smoking, excessive alcohol, chronic sleep deprivation, psychological stress</li>\n      <li>Certain medications that raise glucose (glucocorticoids, thiazides, beta‑blockers, some atypical antipsychotics, statins)</li>\n      <li>Exposure to persistent organic pollutants</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Non‑modifiable risks</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Increasing age and some sex differences</li>\n      <li>Family history/genetic predisposition (heritability ≈72%; common variants such as TCF7L2 increase risk)</li>\n      <li>High‑risk ethnic groups (e.g., Pima, Pacific Islanders, South Asian, Black, Hispanic, Native American)</li>\n      <li>Previous gestational diabetes or related endocrine disorders (PCOS, Cushing’s, acromegaly)</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>Excess body fat explains a large proportion of cases (≈30% in Chinese/Japanese, 60–80% in Europeans/Africans, and ~100% in some Pima/Pacific Islander populations). Intensive lifestyle change (weight loss + activity) substantially reduces progression from prediabetes to type 2 diabetes.</p></div>",
      "visualPrompt": "Concise clinical summary of major causes, drivers, triggers, and risk factors for type 2 diabetes",
